//
// GEMMA4-E2B Q4NX decode throughput vs context length, into
// gemma4_e2b_q4nx.sweep.json (collected by the nightly LLM benchmark).
//
// This is the ONLY throughput measurement this example has. The other q4nx
// examples pair a sweep with run_npu2_profile.lit, which times a real
// prefill+decode turn at the profile prompt's few tokens of context; that needs
// a prefill engine and a token loop, and this example has neither yet. The
// sweep does not: it is decode-only and synthetic, so it is reachable here and
// is what puts this model in the same curve table as its siblings.
//
// Reachable for the same reason it needs no weights: a context is set by SIZING
// the KV cache and telling the device to attend over it, not by prefilling a
// real prompt of that length. So no HF bundle, no hf_token, and no correctness
// claim -- run_npu2_verify.lit is the correctness gate and this one is latency
// only.
//
// FULL DEPTH, not the gate's depth. The layer gate builds one decode wave (or
// two for a KV-shared pair) because it scores a single layer; a sweep built the
// same way would measure a 1/35-scale dispatch and report a number ~35x too
// good. `_compile_decode_build` passes SWEEP_UNI_DEC=35 for exactly this, and
// the engine's Makefile defaults it to the gate's value, so it cannot be left
// implicit.
//
// peano_ge22, not just peano: the repo's pinned llvm-aie (21.0.0) miscompiles
// this model. That is a numerics fault rather than a build failure, so it would
// not stop a latency sweep -- but it would publish a curve for a design that
// returns all-NaN, which is worse than not publishing one.
//
// llvm_link_pre23 is deliberately NOT required, unlike the sibling sweeps. The
// PLE engine's build does not go through fused_decode's inline-attn merge tool:
// instrumenting PATH with a logging llvm-link shim across a full build recorded
// zero calls, and the build succeeds with an LLVM 23 llvm-link first on PATH.
//
// 64k and 128k are swept but allowed to fail. This model's KV BO is ~4.4x
// llama-3.2-1B's per context -- 9.4 GB at 64k, 18.8 GB at 128k -- so whether a
// point is reachable is a property of the host's memory, not of the design. On
// an 89 GB development machine 64k measured 2.06 tok/s and 128k failed; a
// smaller runner will lose 64k too. The point is recorded with its status
// either way rather than reddening CI on someone else's RAM.
//
// REQUIRES: ryzen_ai_npu2, peano, peano_ge22
//
// RUN: mkdir -p test_peano_sweep
// RUN: cd test_peano_sweep
// RUN: %PYTHON %S/../bench/sweep_decode.py --model-name gemma4_e2b_q4nx --makefile %S/Makefile --template-dir %S --bench-model gemma4-e2b --vocab-chunk-i2 27 --n-layers 35 --contexts 1024,2048,4096,8192,16384,32768,65536,131072 --expect-fail 65536,131072 --peano-dir %PEANO_INSTALL_DIR --xrt-dir %XRT_DIR --out gemma4_e2b_q4nx.sweep.json
// RUN: FileCheck %s < gemma4_e2b_q4nx.sweep.json
// CHECK: "model": "gemma4_e2b_q4nx"
// CHECK: "axis": "context_len"
// A parsed number at the reference context, not just a well-formed file: if the
// bench line format drifts out of sweep_decode.py's regex every point goes null
// and the published curve silently empties while the test stays green.
// CHECK: "context_len": 2048
// CHECK-NEXT: "decode_tokens_per_sec": {{[0-9]}}
